---
.title = "Multi-home peers",
.description = "One program reachable over several transports, under one durable name. The id locator names a peer by key and carries its routes as hints, so the routes can change without the peer changing.",
.date = .date("2026-08-24T00:00:00"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

A program reachable over more than one transport can keep its sturdyrefs
(long-lived `ocapn://` references to a published object, explained in
[Concepts]($link.page('concepts'))) working when those transports change.

A standard OCapN locator names one transport and one designator. So a program
that listens on two transports looks like two separate peers, and a sturdyref
written for one of them only works over that one path. glowcap's `id` locator
names the peer by its durable public key instead and lists the paths that reach
it as hints. Its wire names are not part of OCapN yet, so they live in
`glowcap-next`, glowcap's own experimental CapTP version (see [Standing]($link.ref('standing'))).

# One peer, several routes

An `id` locator puts the peer's durable public key in the designator. `id` is
not a transport itself. It bundles routes, and each route is a real transport.
Dialing it tries the routes in order. A route does not identify the peer. The
peer is whoever proves, over the route's channel, that it holds the key the
locator names.

[Dialing one multi-home peer: the first route is down, so the dial moves on, and the next route's channel proves the key. The peer is whoever proves the key. The weak fallback can carry a signed gift, never the identity.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-multihome-dial.svg'))

The routes are written into two hints on a single `ocapn://` URI. Each route
is itself a locator, percent-encoded inside the hint and separated by `|`.
Decoded for reading, the shape is:

```
ocapn://<durable key>.id?via=<route>|<route>&alt-locators=<route>
```

The `via` hint lists the strong routes, the ones where something besides the
address vouches for the durable key:

- [iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')) authenticates its node id in the QUIC
  handshake.
- The [Noise paths]($link.page('transports/noise')) prove the dialed key
  while sealing the stream.
- [WebRTC]($link.page('transports/webrtc')) relies on its signaling service to
  vouch for the peer, so it is as strong as your trust in that service.

A cleartext keyed route is not strong, because its key is only an address to
dial and nothing proves it.

The `alt-locators` hint lists weak fallbacks, such as a plain socket or an
[onion]($link.page('transports/onion')) address. Tor proves an onion service
only to the side that dials it, so an onion route cannot prove a peer in both
directions. A weak route can complete one handoff that a signed gift already
authorized, and nothing more. It never establishes the peer's identity, it
never enters the identity cache, and a sturdyref never dials it.

Each list is capped in length, and the two together have a small size limit.
Every `via` route must name the same key as the designator, so an untrusted
locator cannot cause unbounded work or slip in a second identity.

# What the other peer sees

Routes are a negotiated feature called `multi_location`. glowcap offers it
under its own `glowcap-next` version, and both sides must record it in their
signed hellos (`op:start-session`, the message that opens a session) before
either may use it. A first hello never includes it.

The decision is made per session. One process can use routes with another
glowcap host while its session with a
[Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) vat stays on the
standard version and sees the same wire whether or not multi-homing is in use.

[A negotiated partner receives the id bundle intact. A baseline partner gets one concrete route it can dial, with the private vocabulary stripped, and when no such route exists the give is refused before anything is sent.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-multihome-lowering.svg'))

A handoff can cross between those two kinds of session, because a signed give
travels from the session with the object's host to the
recipient's. The give keeps its
routes only when both sessions negotiated `multi_location`. Otherwise glowcap
gives the recipient one concrete route on a transport that recipient is known
to use, with the private fields removed. If no such route exists, glowcap
refuses the give before sending anything.

# Sturdyrefs that survive a transport change

A sturdyref written against an `id` locator names the peer's key, not one of
its paths. The peer can move to a different transport, or add a second
listener, and stored references keep working. On a Host that has opted into
`multi_location`, redeeming one works like this:

1. If a session already exists whose channel proved that key, on any
   transport, the Host reuses it.
2. Otherwise it tries the strong `via` routes, a few at a time, in the order the
   publisher listed them. When one attempt fails, it starts the next route on
   the list. The first channel that proves the key carries the fetch, and the
   other dials are cancelled.
3. Only after every strong route fails does it try the weak fallbacks, one at
   a time, in their listed order.

The
[multi-home route diagrams](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/08-multihome-routes.md)
draw each of these flows.

# Rooms uses it

Directory peering in [Rooms]($link.page('rooms')) uses multi-homing. A Rooms
server started with `--peering` can list one of its durable rooms in another
Rooms server's directory, the list of rooms people find there, under a single
`id` locator. Its iroh and
Noise listeners, which prove the key, go under `via`. Its onion and plain
socket listeners go under `alt-locators` as weak fallbacks.

Each reader then joins the room over whichever path that reader can open, so
the listing does not pick one transport for everyone. The listing belongs to
the publisher's session with that directory. Revoking or disconnecting it
removes the room from the directory, but a room already handed to a reader
stays live.

# [Standing]($section.id('standing'))

OCapN has not specified multi-homing yet. Its meeting notes discuss ideas from
locators that name several netlayers (OCapN's word for a transport) to a vat id that does not depend on any
transport. Everything described here is built and checked
([ADR-0125](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0125-identity-route-multihome.md)).
The open part is the wire format, which stays a `glowcap-next` extension until
OCapN specifies a compatible one. Until then, peers that do not negotiate it
never see these route hints. [What you can depend on]($link.page('stability'))
lists the checks.
